<!--
  Comb indoor scenario: 40m x 40m floor plan, 3m walls (open top).

  Coordinate system: the spine's centreline is y = 0; the floor spans
  x/y = -20 to +20. At resolution 0.2 that is a 200x200 grid (40k cells),
  deliberately smaller than large_indoor's 250x250 so benchmark runs of
  several strategies stay affordable.

      +20 +----------------------------------------+
          |###..###################################|   `.` free, `#` solid;
          |###..###################################|   one column = 1 m,
          |###..######..###########################|   one row = 2 m, read
          |###..######..###########################|   straight off the
          |###..######..######..##############..###|   truth grid.
          |###..######..######..##############..###|
          |###..######..######..######..######..###|
          |###..######..######..######..######..###|
          |###..######..######..######..######..###|
        0 |........................................|   spine, 3.2 m
          |........................................|
          |#######..######..######..######..#######|
          |#######..######..######..######..#######|
          |#######..######..######..######..#######|
          |###############..######..######..#######|
          |###############..######..######..#######|
          |###############..######..######..#######|
          |###############..######..######..#######|
          |###############..##############..#######|
      -20 |###############..##############..#######|
          -20      -10       0        10       +20

  Rib mouths, west to east, at 4 m pitch, alternating north and south:

      x      -16   -12    -8    -4     0     4     8    12    16
      side     N     S     N     S     N     S     N     S     N
      depth 18.2   5.2  13.2  18.2   9.2  13.2   5.2  18.2   9.2  m

  Nine dead-end ribs open off a central spine, alternating north and south at
  4 m pitch, at four distinct depths (5.2 / 9.2 / 13.2 / 18.2 m). This is an
  ADVERSARIAL benchmark scene, built to discriminate between frontier
  strategies where small_indoor (one open room) and large_indoor (a corridor
  cross with eight through-reachable rooms) do not:

    * A rib has no through-route. A drone that enters must come back out the
      way it came, so a strategy that switches target mid-rib pays the trip
      twice. large_indoor measured one drone retreading 34% of its cells.
    * Two drones in the same rib is pure waste — a 1.2 m corridor has no
      second lane and nothing to divide. A strategy that allocates well sends
      them to different ribs. large_indoor measured one drone covering half
      the map while another never left the corridor junction.
    * The four depths make travel cost the deciding factor between otherwise
      identical candidates, so a strategy that weighs it properly separates
      from one that does not.

  WHY THE MATERIAL BETWEEN RIBS IS SOLID rather than 0.4 m shells around empty
  space: a shell would enclose a sealed void, which `tests/scene_truth.py`
  classifies *free* (no geom covers it) while no drone can ever reach it. That
  reads as missing coverage rather than as wall, and would corrupt every
  coverage and map-accuracy figure the benchmark produces. Solid fill is
  honest: its interior is unobservable exactly the way any wall interior is,
  which the truth grid already accounts for. The perimeter, which has free
  space on one side only, keeps the 0.4 m convention of the other scenes.

  BENCHMARK NOTE: free space is 6272 of the 40,000 cells (15.7%; 258.5 m^2 of
  1600). The rest is solid, and every one of those 6272 cells is reachable from
  the spawn — the scene contains no sealed pocket. Coverage here must be read
  against the *reachable free* cells of the truth grid, not against the cell
  count of the grid: solid interiors are unobservable, so the absolute fraction
  `cli.coverage_fraction` reports is not comparable with large_indoor's.

  DIMENSIONS ARE NOT ARBITRARY. Feature 4c measured that a gap must clear
  `2r + 1` cells PLUS one cell of discretization slop: a wall face landing on a
  cell boundary has its ray hit point attributed to the far cell, so the mapped
  gap is one cell narrower than the real one. At resolution 0.2 with
  clearance_radius 0.20 (r = 1) that floor is 4 cells / 0.8 m. Rib mouths here
  are 6 cells / 1.2 m and the spine is 16 cells / 3.2 m, matching
  large_indoor's doorways and corridors, so the scene survives
  clearance_radius rising to r = 2. A mouth measures 5 or 6 free cells on the
  grid rather than always 6: its faces land exactly on cell boundaries, and
  which side of the tie a boundary cell falls on is decided by float rounding
  in the truth grid. That one cell *is* the slop the floor budgets for, which
  is why the floor is 4 and not 3.

  EVERY WALL FACE LIES ON A 0.2 m LATTICE POINT, which is also a 0.1 m lattice
  point. A face at a half-cell offset is exactly what turned a 3-cell gap into
  2 free cells in the 4c measurement. Geometry was generated from exact
  rational arithmetic (fractions.Fraction) rather than typed, and
  tests/unit/test_simulation/test_comb_indoor.py re-asserts the lattice
  invariant — and the rib mouth widths, and every rib tip's reachability —
  against the parsed model.

  Box geom sizes are half-extents. The perimeter is 0.4 m thick (half-extent
  0.2).

  Drones are injected programmatically by SimulationEngine —
  do not define drone bodies here.

  Rendering follows large_indoor.xml: open top (no ceiling geom) so --view
  looks down into the plan, opaque walls, two directional lights. The camera
  sits steeper than the other scenes because a comb only reads from above.
-->
<mujoco model="comb_indoor">
  <option timestep="0.01" gravity="0 0 -9.81"/>

  <!-- Default free-camera framing, as in the other scenes: the passive viewer
       otherwise opens at floor level. -->
  <statistic center="0 0 1" extent="36"/>
  <visual>
    <headlight ambient="0.5 0.5 0.5" diffuse="0.45 0.45 0.45" specular="0 0 0"/>
    <global azimuth="90" elevation="-75"/>
  </visual>

  <worldbody>
    <!-- Directional lights: no positional falloff, so a 40 m floor has no
         hotspot. Lights never collide — no effect on ray-casts or the map. -->
    <light name="key" directional="true" pos="0 0 20" dir="0.2 0.3 -1"
           diffuse="0.55 0.55 0.55" specular="0 0 0"/>
    <light name="fill" directional="true" pos="12 12 20" dir="-0.5 -0.5 -1"
           diffuse="0.35 0.35 0.35" specular="0 0 0"/>

    <!-- Floor -->
    <geom name="floor" type="plane" size="20 20 0.05"
          rgba="0.75 0.75 0.78 1.0"/>

    <!-- Perimeter (generated; see the header). Straddles the floor edge, so
         the grid's outermost ring of cells is wall. -->
    <geom name="wall_north" type="box" pos="0 20 1.5"
          size="20 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="wall_south" type="box" pos="0 -20 1.5"
          size="20 0.2 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="wall_east" type="box" pos="20 0 1.5"
          size="0.2 20 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="wall_west" type="box" pos="-20 0 1.5"
          size="0.2 20 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>

    <!-- North fill: the slab between the spine (y = 1.6) and the perimeter
         (y = 19.8), minus the rib slots. Its south face is the spine's north
         wall; the gaps in it are the rib mouths. -->
    <geom name="fill_n_1" type="box" pos="-18.2 10.7 1.5"
          size="1.6 9.1 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="fill_n_2" type="box" pos="-12 10.7 1.5"
          size="3.4 9.1 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="fill_n_3" type="box" pos="-4 10.7 1.5"
          size="3.4 9.1 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="fill_n_4" type="box" pos="4 10.7 1.5"
          size="3.4 9.1 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="fill_n_5" type="box" pos="12 10.7 1.5"
          size="3.4 9.1 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="fill_n_6" type="box" pos="18.2 10.7 1.5"
          size="1.6 9.1 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>

    <!-- North caps: solid beyond a rib's tip. The rib at x = -16 runs the
         full 18.2 m to the perimeter and needs none. -->
    <geom name="cap_n_2" type="box" pos="-8 17.3 1.5"
          size="0.6 2.5 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="cap_n_3" type="box" pos="0 15.3 1.5"
          size="0.6 4.5 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="cap_n_4" type="box" pos="8 13.3 1.5"
          size="0.6 6.5 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="cap_n_5" type="box" pos="16 15.3 1.5"
          size="0.6 4.5 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>

    <!-- South fill: the same slab mirrored about the spine. -->
    <geom name="fill_s_1" type="box" pos="-16.2 -10.7 1.5"
          size="3.6 9.1 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="fill_s_2" type="box" pos="-8 -10.7 1.5"
          size="3.4 9.1 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="fill_s_3" type="box" pos="0 -10.7 1.5"
          size="3.4 9.1 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="fill_s_4" type="box" pos="8 -10.7 1.5"
          size="3.4 9.1 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="fill_s_5" type="box" pos="16.2 -10.7 1.5"
          size="3.6 9.1 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>

    <!-- South caps. The ribs at x = -4 and x = 12 run the full depth. -->
    <geom name="cap_s_1" type="box" pos="-12 -13.3 1.5"
          size="0.6 6.5 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
    <geom name="cap_s_3" type="box" pos="4 -17.3 1.5"
          size="0.6 2.5 1.5" rgba="0.55 0.58 0.65 1.0"/>
  </worldbody>
</mujoco>
